## User question:
How to configure Nokia routers to allow LLGR stale routes to be advertised to all peers, enabling them to exit the local AS?
---
## Proposed answer:
To configure Nokia routers to allow LLGR stale routes to be advertised to all peers, enabling them to exit the local AS, use the following CLI commands:

1. Enable long-lived graceful restart at the global BGP level:
```
configure router bgp graceful-restart long-lived
```

2. Configure the specific address families where you want to apply LLGR (repeat for each needed family):
```
family ipv4
family ipv6
family vpn-ipv4
family vpn-ipv6
exit
```

3. Apply these settings to all BGP groups/peers where you want to advertise LLGR stale routes:
```
group <group-name>
    graceful-restart long-lived
        family ipv4
        family ipv6
        family vpn-ipv4
        family vpn-ipv6
        exit
    exit
```

These commands will enable the advertisement of LLGR stale routes to all peers, allowing them to be propagated outside the local AS.
---
## Router configuration manual:
CLIs:
advertise-stale-to-all-neighbors [ without-no-export | no without-no-export ]
no advertise-stale-to-all-neighbors
FuncDef:
This command allows BGP routes marked as LLGR stale to be advertised to BGP peers that did not advertise the LLGR capability when the session was opened. Thenoversion of this command causes advertisement behavior to follow the rule that stale routes cannot be advertised to a peer that does not understand or implement the LLGR capability. Stale routes are withdrawn towards such peers.
ParaDef:
{'Parameters': 'without-no-export', 'Info': 'Allows LLGR stale routes to be advertised to all peers, such that they can exit the local AS.'}
