Generate CLI configuration commands that fulfill the user's request using the information from the reference content. Please respond directly to the user's question. Do not mention phrases like "based on the information you provided", "according to the documentation", or anything similar.
To tie the BGP neighbor session state with the underlying BFD session status on a Nokia router, use the following CLI configuration commands:

```
configure router bgp
    group <group-name>
        neighbor <ip-address>
            bfd-enable
            exit
        exit
    exit
exit
```

Alternatively, you can apply this at the BGP group level if all neighbors in the group should inherit the setting:

```
configure router bgp
    group <group-name>
        bfd-enable
        exit
    exit
exit
```

This configuration ensures the BGP session state follows the BFD session status. If the BFD session goes down, the BGP session will also be brought down immediately rather than waiting for the BGP hold timer to expire.
------------
As a network device expert, please determine whether the following answer correctly responds to the user's question based on the router configuration manual. Only output "Correct" or "Incorrect" without any explanation or additional information. You must make your own judgment based on the Router configuration manual provided to you, not on your own knowledge.
---
## User question:
"How to configure a Nokia router to tie the BGP neighbor session state with the underlying BFD session status?"
---
## Proposed answer:
To tie the BGP neighbor session state with the underlying BFD session status on a Nokia router, use the following CLI configuration commands:

```
configure router bgp
    group <group-name>
        neighbor <ip-address>
            bfd-enable
            exit
        exit
    exit
exit
```

Alternatively, you can apply this at the BGP group level if all neighbors in the group should inherit the setting:

```
configure router bgp
    group <group-name>
        bfd-enable
        exit
    exit
exit
```

This configuration ensures the BGP session state follows the BFD session status. If the BFD session goes down, the BGP session will also be brought down immediately rather than waiting for the BGP hold timer to expire.
---
## Router configuration manual:
CLIs:
bfd-enable
no bfd-enable
FuncDef:
This command enables the use of bi-directional forwarding (BFD) to control the state of the associated protocol interface. By enabling BFD on a given protocol interface, the state of the protocol interface is tied to the state of the BFD session between the local node and the remote node. The parameters used for the BFD are set via the BFD command under the IP interface.
ParaDef:
No ParaDef found
